#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/entity-ratchet -- plan_entity.md step 8c: the census, the three gates
and the crossing budget, as one `make test` leg.

    tools/entity-ratchet              measure and compare; non-zero on regression
    tools/entity-ratchet --write      re-take docs/entity-ratchet.tsv (LOWER only)
    tools/entity-ratchet --write --allow-regression
                                      ... and let a row RISE, deliberately
    tools/entity-ratchet --self-test  check that this script can fail

WHY THIS IS NOT "tools/entity-gates EXITS 0"
============================================

tools/entity-gates answers "is the port finished?" and its own header says
every gate FAILS today, by design. A leg wired to its exit code would be red
from the day it landed until the day the port ends, which is a leg nobody can
keep green and therefore a leg everybody learns to ignore. That is not a gate,
it is a broken window.

tools/entity-census is the opposite shape: it fails on ANY drift, up or down,
and is re-taken with --write. That one IS usable as a leg and is run as-is.

So the gates become a RATCHET instead. Every gated row of `entity-gates --tsv`
is recorded in docs/entity-ratchet.tsv, and the leg fails when a row goes UP.
Red on regression, green on progress:

    server 32 -> 33 casts     FAIL   a cast came back
    server 32 -> 31 casts     pass   and --write records the new floor

That is the direction plan_entity.md §5 asks for -- "each phase may only lower
it" -- applied to the three gates rather than only to the crossing budget.

WHAT STOPS THE RATCHET BEING WOUND BACK
=======================================

A ratchet whose baseline anybody can raise is a comment. Three things:

  * `--write` REFUSES to raise a row. Lowering needs nothing; raising needs
    `--allow-regression` on the command line, which is one grep away in a diff.
  * A row `entity-gates` produces and the baseline does not name FAILS, and so
    does a row the baseline names and the gates did not produce. So a regression
    cannot be hidden by deleting its row, and a gate that silently stopped being
    measured is not a pass. (This is also what stops somebody lowering the leg's
    floor by shortening the baseline: tools/leg-floor sizes this leg from the
    baseline's row count, and a shorter baseline is a failing run.)
  * BOTH SIDES are compared. A check that only looked at the server column
    would be half a check, and every row here has two numbers.

WHAT THIS LEG DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
======================================

It does NOT run the live gate and it does NOT enforce a crossing COUNT. It
cannot: a crossing count needs a game, a map and five minutes, and
tools/live-gate-budget.tsv's own header records that the same unchanged build
measured `projectile` at 35.349 and at 221.043 per frame an hour apart -- 6.3x,
on every rate row together. A budget that cannot resolve a regression under 2x
does not belong on a hair trigger in `make test`, and three live-gate steps are
red today for known, root-caused reasons (STATE.md: 7a's dust row, 7e's
soundscapes, the residual prop-fall). A leg that went red for those would be
re-run until it passed, which is how a gate becomes a coin flip.

What it enforces about the budget is everything that does NOT need a game --
the file's own arithmetic and its agreement with tools/live-gate:

  * every stage tools/live-gate runs has a row, and every row is a stage it runs
  * ceiling_per_frame is exactly recorded_per_frame*1.5+10
  * ceiling_total is NOT ABOVE total_max*1.5+200, clamped up by SHORT_STAGE_FLOOR
    -- this is the one that catches a ceiling widened by hand, which is the
    only way §5's ratchet gets defeated without a measurement
  * recorded_per_frame really is recorded_total/recorded_frames
  * gated_on agrees with recorded_frames against MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE
  * total_min <= recorded_total <= total_max, samples >= 1, and no ceiling below
    the largest total the row itself records

EVERY CONSTANT IN THAT LIST IS IMPORTED FROM tools/live-gate, never copied.
1.5, 10, 200, 20, 4 and SHORT_STAGE_FLOOR are read out of that module at run
time, so the two files cannot drift apart -- and the self-test asserts the
values it got are the values the source spells, from a second, independent
reading of the file.

WHY A BUILT TREE IS REQUIRED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A SKIP
========================================================

entity-gates and entity-census read game/mod_sharp/bin/linux64/*.so and the
CMake build (build/compile_commands.json, build/build.ninja), in
farm/<config> (see FARM below).
Both are build output; a checkout that has never been built has neither. So
does the `native` leg (it cannot link without the farm's
src/lib/public/linux64/tier1.a), and this leg behaves the same way:
it FAILS, loudly, naming the build command. It never reports NOT MEASURED and
it never exits 0 having measured nothing -- a tool that answers "clean" to an
unbuilt tree is the false green tools/run-test-legs exists to refuse.

EXIT 1 FROM entity-gates IS EXPECTED AND IS NOT AN ERROR. Its header defines
three exit codes: 0 all gates pass, 1 a gate failed, 2 could not measure. Only
2 is fatal here. Reading 1 as a failure is the mistake this whole file avoids.
"""
import collections
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import io
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
ROOT = os.path.abspath(os.path.join(HERE, '..'))
sys.path.insert(0, HERE)
import cmakegraph  # noqa: E402

# The tree that is MEASURED is not this one. entity-gates and entity-census read
# build/ (the CMake binary dir) and game/mod_sharp/bin/linux64 relative to where
# they run -- the build's own view -- and only the farm has that view:
# farm/<config>/build is where CMake and ninja ran, and its game/mod_sharp is
# the compat link to bin/<config>/mods/test. So they run there, and the deploy
# edges are resolved there. The baseline, the tools and live-gate are this
# repo's. The baseline was measured on debug; SOURCESHARP_CONFIG=release
# measures farm/release, whose -O2 counts are not the baseline's.
CONFIG = os.environ.get('SOURCESHARP_CONFIG') or 'debug'
FARM = os.path.join(ROOT, 'farm', CONFIG)

BASELINE = 'docs/entity-ratchet.tsv'
GATES = 'tools/entity-gates'
BUILD = 'build'       # the CMake binary dir, relative to FARM
CENSUS = 'tools/entity-census'
LIVE_GATE = 'tools/live-gate'

# Roles entity-gates emits. `verdict` rows are derived from the others and
# carry no numbers, so they are not baselined; `gate` and `reconcile` both are.
# reconcile rows are ratcheted too: they are the SAME population read a looser
# way, so one of them rising is the same regression seen through a wider lens.
RATCHETED_ROLES = ('gate', 'reconcile')


class CannotMeasure(Exception):
    """Nothing was measured. Never report a clean tree from one of these."""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tools/live-gate, imported rather than re-typed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def load_live_gate(path=None):
    path = path or os.path.join(ROOT, LIVE_GATE)
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        raise CannotMeasure('no %s -- the crossing budget has no owner to '
                            'check it against' % LIVE_GATE)
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader('_ss_live_gate', path)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader('_ss_live_gate', loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    try:
        loader.exec_module(module)
    except Exception as exc:                       # pragma: no cover - defensive
        raise CannotMeasure('cannot import %s (%s)' % (LIVE_GATE, exc))
    for want in ('MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE', 'SHORT_STAGE_FLOOR', 'ceiling_for',
                 'ceiling_total_for', 'read_budget', 'ALL_STEPS', 'BUDGET'):
        if not hasattr(module, want):
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s no longer defines %s. The budget checks are built out of '
                'that module on purpose -- a copy of the formula here would '
                'drift from the one that records it.' % (LIVE_GATE, want))
    return module


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH BUILD ARE THESE COUNTS OFF? -- the precondition, and it is not academic.
#
# entity-gates and entity-census read the TU list from the DEBUG object table
# and the nm counts from game/mod_sharp/bin/linux64/*.so. Those are two
# different things and nothing made them agree. In the old single tree
# `src/buildsourcesharp release` deployed to the SAME directory, at -O2, and
# stripped the debug info out of what it left there -- so a release build
# followed by `make test` would compare
# release nm counts against a debug baseline and against a debug TU list, and
# the leg would go red with hundreds of movement in rows nobody touched.
#
# That is a MISLEADING RED, which this project has already paid for once: the
# live gate's failures were being misattributed for a week (STATE.md). A red
# that names the wrong cause is worse than a red that says "I could not
# measure", so this says the latter.
#
# The build itself carries the answer -- each DLL's link edge in build.ninja
# names the file it links (build/out/server.so) and its POST_BUILD deploy step
# the directory it copies it to -- so the deployed file and the file the object
# table describes are compared by GNU build-id, which is exact and costs
# nothing (readelf -n reads only the note sections). Each configuration has its
# own build dir and its own bin/<config> now, so the old failure (a release
# build deployed over the debug one) cannot happen the old way; the check
# stays, because "the DLL under test is the one this object table describes"
# is still the precondition and a stale deploy still breaks it.
#
# EVERY deployed target is checked, not only the two DLLs the gates read. All
# three come out of one build, so one of them disagreeing means the deployed
# tree is not this build -- and picking out "the two that matter" would mean a
# second, hand-written copy of the paths entity-gates already defaults to,
# which is the drift a single source of truth exists to prevent.
#
# It RAISES rather than reporting a failed check. Every number after it would
# be measured on the wrong binary, so reporting them at all would be inventing
# evidence. And it is deliberately NOT also printed as a passing row: a row
# that can only ever be green is the check-that-cannot-fail this tree has found
# two dozen of.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEPLOYED_TARGETS = (cmakegraph.SERVER, cmakegraph.CLIENT, cmakegraph.HOST)


def deploy_edges(bdir):
    """-> [(deployed path, linked path)], absolute, one per deployed target.

    A target whose link edge or deploy step cannot be read is refused: a
    MISSING deploy is exactly what this check exists to catch.
    """
    try:
        return [(cmakegraph.deployed_so(t, bdir), cmakegraph.link_edge(t, bdir)['output'])
                for t in DEPLOYED_TARGETS]
    except cmakegraph.GraphError as exc:
        raise CannotMeasure('%s. Build first:  make debug' % exc)


def build_id(path):
    """The GNU build-id of an ELF file, or None if it has none."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(['readelf', '-n', path], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, universal_newlines=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise CannotMeasure('cannot run readelf (%s)' % exc)
    found = re.search(r'Build ID: ([0-9a-f]+)', proc.stdout)
    return found.group(1) if found else None


def check_deployed_is_this_build(edges, id_of):
    """-> how many binaries agreed. Anything else raises."""
    if not edges:
        raise CannotMeasure(
            'no deploy edge in %s/build.ninja, so nothing here can tell which '
            'build the DLLs under test came from. An unchecked deployment is how a '
            'release binary gets compared against a debug baseline.' % BUILD)
    for deployed, linked in edges:
        theirs, ours = id_of(deployed), id_of(linked)
        if ours is None:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s has no GNU build-id, so the deployed binary cannot be '
                'identified. Build first:  make debug' % linked)
        if theirs is None:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s has no GNU build-id -- it is missing, stripped past its '
                'notes, or not an ELF file at all. Build first:  '
                'make debug' % deployed)
        if theirs != ours:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s (build-id %s) is NOT the binary %s describes (%s). The '
                'gates read the TU list from this build and the symbol '
                'counts from the deployed file, so every count would be off a '
                'different build -- a stale or foreign deploy. Re-deploy:  '
                'make debug'
                % (deployed, theirs[:12], linked, ours[:12]))
    return len(edges)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The gate rows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GateRow = collections.namedtuple('GateRow', 'gate role metric srv cli')


def parse_gate_tsv(text):
    """`entity-gates --tsv` output -> {(gate, role, metric): GateRow}.

    A LINE THIS CANNOT PARSE IS AN ERROR, never a skip. tools/live-gate's own
    budget reader records why: the reader that skipped a row it did not
    understand turned a schema change into an EMPTY budget that gated nothing
    and still exited 0. Same trap, same treatment.
    """
    rows = {}
    for number, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        line = line.rstrip('\n')
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        parts = line.split('\t')
        if len(parts) != 6:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s --tsv line %d has %d column(s), not 6: %r. The row schema '
                'is gate/role/metric/server/client/verdict; a line this cannot '
                'read is refused rather than skipped, because a reader that '
                'skips what it does not understand reports an empty tree as a '
                'clean one.' % (GATES, number, len(parts), line))
        gate, role, metric, srv, cli, _verdict = parts
        if role not in RATCHETED_ROLES:
            continue
        try:
            row = GateRow(gate, role, metric, int(srv), int(cli))
        except ValueError:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s --tsv line %d has a non-numeric count: %r' % (GATES, number, line))
        key = (gate, role, metric)
        if key in rows:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s --tsv names the row %r twice. The baseline is keyed on it, '
                'so a duplicate would silently ratchet only one of the two.'
                % (GATES, key))
        rows[key] = row
    return rows


def run_gates(argv=None):
    """The production measurement. Exit 1 is a FAILING GATE, not an error."""
    cmd = [os.path.join(ROOT, GATES), '--tsv'] + list(argv or [])
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=FARM, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, universal_newlines=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise CannotMeasure('cannot run %s (%s)' % (GATES, exc))
    if proc.returncode not in (0, 1):
        raise CannotMeasure(
            '%s exited %d (its exit 2 means "nothing was measured"):\n%s\n'
            'Build first:  make debug'
            % (GATES, proc.returncode, proc.stderr.strip()))
    rows = parse_gate_tsv(proc.stdout)
    if not rows:
        raise CannotMeasure(
            '%s --tsv produced no gated rows at all. Every comparison below '
            'would then pass for the reason an empty scan is always clean.'
            % GATES)
    return rows


def read_baseline(path):
    text = None
    try:
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as handle:
            text = handle.read()
    except OSError as exc:
        raise CannotMeasure(
            'no ratchet baseline at %s (%s). Take one with '
            '`tools/entity-ratchet --write`.' % (path, exc))
    rows = {}
    for number, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        line = line.rstrip('\n')
        if not line.strip() or line.startswith('#'):
            continue
        parts = line.split('\t')
        if len(parts) != 5:
            raise CannotMeasure(
                '%s line %d has %d column(s), not 5: %r' % (path, number, len(parts), line))
        gate, role, metric, srv, cli = parts
        try:
            rows[(gate, role, metric)] = GateRow(gate, role, metric, int(srv), int(cli))
        except ValueError:
            raise CannotMeasure('%s line %d has a non-numeric count: %r'
                                % (path, number, line))
    if not rows:
        raise CannotMeasure(
            '%s names no rows. An empty baseline makes every comparison below '
            'vacuous, so it is refused rather than treated as "nothing to '
            'check".' % path)
    return rows


BASELINE_HEADER = """\
# docs/entity-ratchet.tsv -- the RATCHET FLOOR for plan_entity.md's three gates.
#
# Written by `tools/entity-ratchet --write`, checked by `tools/entity-ratchet`,
# run as the `entity` leg of `make test`. Read that tool's header first.
#
# One row per gated (and per reconciliation) row of `tools/entity-gates --tsv`.
# The leg FAILS when a number here goes UP, and passes when it goes down --
# plan_entity.md §5's "each phase may only lower it", applied to the gates.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE IT
#   you made a number smaller   tools/entity-ratchet --write     (no ceremony)
#   you made a number BIGGER    that is the regression this file exists to
#                               catch. `--write` refuses; `--write
#                               --allow-regression` does it and the diff shows
#                               exactly which row you widened and by how much.
#
# MERGING TWO LANES' VERSIONS OF THIS FILE: do not resolve it by hand and do
# not keep both. It is a MEASUREMENT, not a list of claims -- the shape the
# lane brief warns about, where two lanes each move one number and keep-both
# leaves a row that was never true of any tree. Take the merge, build, and run
# `tools/entity-ratchet --write`; the tool refuses to write a row that went up,
# so a resolution that hid a regression cannot be committed by accident.
#
# These are the numbers the port drives to ZERO. Every one of them reaching 0
# is the definition of done, and on that day `tools/entity-gates` exits 0 and
# this file is a list of zeroes.
#
# columns:
# gate\trole\tmetric\tserver\tclient
"""


def write_baseline(path, rows):
    order = sorted(rows.values(), key=lambda r: (r.gate, r.role, r.metric))
    with open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as handle:
        handle.write(BASELINE_HEADER)
        for row in order:
            handle.write('%s\t%s\t%s\t%d\t%d\n'
                         % (row.gate, row.role, row.metric, row.srv, row.cli))


def gate_checks(observed, baseline):
    """-> [(label, ok, detail)], one per row plus the two set checks. PURE.

    Both sides of every row are compared. A row present on one side only is a
    failure with a name, not a silently dropped comparison.
    """
    out = []

    missing = sorted(set(baseline) - set(observed))
    out.append((
        'every baselined row was measured', not missing,
        'the baseline names %d row(s) %s did not produce: %s'
        % (len(missing), GATES, '; '.join('%s/%s/%s' % k for k in missing[:6]))
        if missing else '%d rows' % len(baseline)))

    extra = sorted(set(observed) - set(baseline))
    out.append((
        'every measured row is baselined', not extra,
        '%s produced %d row(s) the baseline does not name, so nothing '
        'ratchets them: %s. Re-take with --write.'
        % (GATES, len(extra), '; '.join('%s/%s/%s' % k for k in extra[:6]))
        if extra else '%d rows' % len(observed)))

    for key in sorted(set(observed) & set(baseline)):
        now, was = observed[key], baseline[key]
        rose = []
        if now.srv > was.srv:
            rose.append('server %d -> %d (+%d)' % (was.srv, now.srv, now.srv - was.srv))
        if now.cli > was.cli:
            rose.append('client %d -> %d (+%d)' % (was.cli, now.cli, now.cli - was.cli))
        label = 'gate %s %s: %s' % (key[0], key[1], key[2])
        if rose:
            out.append((label, False,
                        'ROSE -- ' + ', '.join(rose)
                        + '. A gated number may only go DOWN (plan_entity.md §5).'))
        elif now.srv < was.srv or now.cli < was.cli:
            out.append((label, True,
                        'improved %d/%d -> %d/%d; re-take with --write'
                        % (was.srv, was.cli, now.srv, now.cli)))
        else:
            out.append((label, True, 'held at %d/%d' % (now.srv, now.cli)))
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The crossing budget: the half that needs no game.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Consts = collections.namedtuple(
    'Consts', 'min_frames_for_rate short_stage_floor ceiling_for ceiling_total_for')


def consts_from(module):
    return Consts(module.MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE, dict(module.SHORT_STAGE_FLOOR),
                  module.ceiling_for, module.ceiling_total_for)


def budget_row_issues(stage, row, consts):
    """Everything wrong with one budget row, as sentences. PURE.

    The formula cap is the load-bearing one: `ceiling_total` may sit BELOW
    total_max*1.5+200 (tools/live-gate keeps an existing ceiling when a raise
    is unearned) but it may never sit above it, because that is a limit
    relaxed rather than a measurement corrected -- exactly what §5 forbids.
    """
    bad = []
    if row.frames < 1:
        bad.append('recorded_frames %d' % row.frames)
        return bad
    if row.samples < 1:
        bad.append('samples %d: a row resting on no measurement' % row.samples)
    want_gated = 'rate' if row.frames >= consts.min_frames_for_rate else 'total'
    if row.gated_on != want_gated:
        bad.append('gated_on=%s on %d frames; MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE=%d says %s'
                   % (row.gated_on, row.frames, consts.min_frames_for_rate, want_gated))
    rate = round(row.total / float(row.frames), 3)
    if round(row.rate, 3) != rate:
        bad.append('recorded_per_frame %s but %d/%d = %s'
                   % (row.rate, row.total, row.frames, rate))
    # THE FORMULA IS APPLIED TO THE FULL-PRECISION RATE, not to the rounded
    # one in the file. tools/live-gate computes ceiling_for(rate) from
    # total/frames and only THEN rounds `rate` to three places for the column,
    # so four of today's twenty rows differ in the last decimal from the
    # ceiling the rounded column would give -- hot-reload, map-reload,
    # save-load and second-client. Comparing against the rounded column would
    # make this check fail on an untouched file, which is how a real invariant
    # gets deleted. TOLERANCE 0.001: one unit in the last recorded place. A
    # ceiling widened by hand moves by tens, never by a thousandth.
    want_rate_ceiling = consts.ceiling_for(row.total / float(row.frames))
    if abs(row.ceiling_rate - want_rate_ceiling) > 0.001:
        bad.append('ceiling_per_frame %s but the formula on %d/%d gives %s'
                   % (row.ceiling_rate, row.total, row.frames,
                      round(want_rate_ceiling, 3)))
    cap = consts.ceiling_total_for(max(row.total_max,
                                       consts.short_stage_floor.get(stage, 0)))
    if row.ceiling_total > cap:
        bad.append('ceiling_total %d is ABOVE the %d the formula allows for '
                   'total_max=%d -- a limit relaxed, not a measurement corrected'
                   % (row.ceiling_total, cap, row.total_max))
    if row.ceiling_total < row.total_max:
        bad.append('ceiling_total %d is below total_max %d -- the row fails on '
                   'its own recorded sample' % (row.ceiling_total, row.total_max))
    if not row.total_min <= row.total <= row.total_max:
        bad.append('recorded_total %d is outside total_min..total_max %d..%d'
                   % (row.total, row.total_min, row.total_max))
    return bad


def budget_checks(budget, stages, consts):
    """-> [(label, ok, detail)], the two set checks plus one per row. PURE."""
    out = []
    missing = [s for s in stages if s not in budget]
    out.append((
        'every live-gate stage has a budget row', not missing,
        'tools/live-gate runs %s with no row in the budget, so they are '
        'ungated' % ', '.join(missing) if missing
        else '%d stages' % len(stages)))
    extra = [s for s in sorted(budget) if s not in stages]
    out.append((
        'every budget row is a live-gate stage', not extra,
        'the budget gates %s, which tools/live-gate does not run' % ', '.join(extra)
        if extra else '%d rows' % len(budget)))
    for stage in sorted(budget):
        issues = budget_row_issues(stage, budget[stage], consts)
        out.append(('budget %s' % stage, not issues,
                    '; '.join(issues) if issues
                    else 'gated_on=%s, ceiling %s/frame, %d total'
                         % (budget[stage].gated_on, budget[stage].ceiling_rate,
                            budget[stage].ceiling_total)))
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The census: run as-is. It is already a diff against a committed baseline.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE ONE PART OF THE CENSUS CI CANNOT REPRODUCE. The baseline is taken on the
# fork, and its "... their lines" rows count the text of the stock (Valve)
# TUs, which carry the fork's edits. CI sets ROOT up from the public patch
# series (make sdk-upstream), which does not carry all of them, so on a runner
# those rows read fewer lines while every other row agrees. With SOURCESHARP_CI
# set (the workflow sets it; nothing else does), drift confined to those rows
# is reported and passed; drift in any other row still fails. The same rule as
# [NotOnCiFact] and SS_SKIP_ON_CI, and the same variable, read the same way.
def on_ci(environ=None):
    return (environ if environ is not None else os.environ).get('SOURCESHARP_CI') == '1'


def only_stock_line_counts_moved(detail):
    """True when every changed row of a census diff is a "... their lines" row."""
    changed = [line for line in detail
               if line[:1] in ('-', '+') and not line.startswith(('---', '+++'))]
    return bool(changed) and all('... their lines' in line for line in changed)


def census_check(environ=None):
    cmd = [os.path.join(ROOT, CENSUS)]
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=FARM, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, universal_newlines=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise CannotMeasure('cannot run %s (%s)' % (CENSUS, exc))
    if proc.returncode == 2:
        raise CannotMeasure('%s could not measure: %s\nBuild first:  '
                            'make debug'
                            % (CENSUS, proc.stderr.strip()))
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return [('census agrees with docs/entity-census.txt', True,
                 proc.stdout.strip().splitlines()[-1] if proc.stdout.strip() else 'exit 0')]
    detail = (proc.stdout + proc.stderr).strip().splitlines()
    if on_ci(environ) and only_stock_line_counts_moved(detail):
        return [('census agrees with docs/entity-census.txt', True,
                 'SKIPPED ON CI (SOURCESHARP_CI): only the stock TUs\' line counts '
                 'moved, and they count the fork\'s edits to Valve files, which the '
                 'public patch series CI sets up from does not carry; every other row '
                 'was compared')]
    if len(detail) > 30:
        detail = detail[:30] + ['... %d more lines; run `make entity-census`'
                                % (len(detail) - 30)]
    return [('census agrees with docs/entity-census.txt', False,
             '\n      '.join(detail))]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def report(results, out=sys.stdout):
    failed = 0
    for label, ok, detail in results:
        out.write('  %-5s %s%s\n' % ('ok' if ok else 'FAIL', label,
                                     ('   -- ' + detail) if detail else ''))
        if not ok:
            failed += 1
    out.write('  ==> %d checks, %d failed\n' % (len(results), failed))
    return failed


def measure():
    """Every section runs, whatever the ones before it did."""
    live = load_live_gate()
    agreed = check_deployed_is_this_build(deploy_edges(os.path.join(FARM, BUILD)), build_id)
    print('    %d deployed DLL(s) carry the build-id %s/build.ninja describes'
          % (agreed, BUILD))
    results = []
    results += census_check()
    results += gate_checks(run_gates(), read_baseline(os.path.join(ROOT, BASELINE)))
    results += budget_checks(live.read_budget(live.BUDGET),
                             [s[0] for s in live.ALL_STEPS], consts_from(live))
    return results


def do_write(allow_regression):
    path = os.path.join(ROOT, BASELINE)
    observed = run_gates()
    try:
        old = read_baseline(path)
    except CannotMeasure:
        old = {}
    rose = []
    for key, row in sorted(observed.items()):
        was = old.get(key)
        if was is None:
            continue
        if row.srv > was.srv or row.cli > was.cli:
            rose.append('%s/%s/%s  %d/%d -> %d/%d'
                        % (key[0], key[1], key[2], was.srv, was.cli, row.srv, row.cli))
    if rose and not allow_regression:
        sys.stderr.write(
            'entity-ratchet: REFUSING to write. %d row(s) would go UP:\n'
            % len(rose))
        for line in rose:
            sys.stderr.write('    %s\n' % line)
        sys.stderr.write(
            '  A gated number going up is the regression this file exists to\n'
            '  catch (plan_entity.md §5: each phase may only lower it). If the\n'
            '  rise is real and intended, say so on the command line:\n'
            '      tools/entity-ratchet --write --allow-regression\n'
            '  and the diff will show exactly which row you widened.\n')
        return 1
    write_baseline(path, observed)
    print('entity-ratchet: wrote %s (%d rows%s)'
          % (BASELINE, len(observed),
             ', %d RAISED deliberately' % len(rose) if rose else ''))
    return 0


def main(argv):
    os.chdir(FARM)
    allow = '--allow-regression' in argv
    argv = [a for a in argv if a != '--allow-regression']
    if argv and argv[0] in ('-h', '--help'):
        sys.stderr.write(__doc__.split('\n\n')[0].strip() + '\n')
        return 2
    if argv and argv[0] == '--write':
        try:
            return do_write(allow)
        except CannotMeasure as exc:
            sys.stderr.write('entity-ratchet: %s\n' % exc)
            return 2
    if argv:
        sys.stderr.write('entity-ratchet: unknown argument %r\n' % argv[0])
        return 2
    print('==> tools/entity-ratchet -- census, the three gates, the crossing budget')
    try:
        results = measure()
    except CannotMeasure as exc:
        # NOT a skip, NOT a zero-check pass. tools/run-test-legs would read a
        # silent exit as NO TESTS; this says what went wrong and fails.
        sys.stderr.write('entity-ratchet: NOTHING WAS MEASURED: %s\n' % exc)
        return 2
    return 1 if report(results) else 0


# ===========================================================================
# Self-test.
#
# The dangerous result for a ratchet is a PASS, so every check below is
# written as a pair: the good input passes, and one deliberately broken input
# FAILS. A comparison that only ever saw agreeing numbers would be green on a
# tree with a cast planted in it, which is the one thing step 8c's gate asks
# this leg to catch.
#
# The fixture transcripts are CAPTURED, not printed. report() writes lines
# that look exactly like the production ones, and a fixture's "3 checks, 2
# failed" left on stdout would be summed into this leg's own count by
# tools/run-test-legs -- the fixture-summary bug tools/leg-floor and
# tools/checkers both carry a guard against. The last check here is that guard.
# ===========================================================================
def self_test():
    import tempfile

    checks = failed = 0
    transcripts = []

    def ck(label, cond, detail=''):
        nonlocal checks, failed
        checks += 1
        print('  %-5s %s%s' % ('ok' if cond else 'FAIL', label,
                               ('   -- ' + detail) if detail else ''))
        if not cond:
            failed += 1

    def quiet(results):
        """report() a fixture's results with stdout HELD BACK, and hand the
        results straight back so a check can look at them."""
        buf = io.StringIO()
        report(results, out=buf)
        transcripts.append(buf.getvalue())
        return results

    def fails(results):
        return [(l, d) for l, ok, d in results if not ok]

    def refused(fn, *a):
        try:
            fn(*a)
        except CannotMeasure:
            return True
        return False

    print('==> tools/entity-ratchet self-test')

    # -- the CI rule for the census's fork-text rows -------------------------
    LINES_ONLY = ['--- docs/entity-census.txt', '+++ (measured now)', '@@ -9,9 +9,9 @@',
                  ' stock TUs that use an entity type but define none    0     4',
                  '-  ... their lines                                    0  5634',
                  '+  ... their lines                                    0  5082']
    ck('a diff whose only changed rows are stock line counts is recognised',
       only_stock_line_counts_moved(LINES_ONLY))
    ck('one that also moves a count row is not -- that drift still fails on CI',
       not only_stock_line_counts_moved(LINES_ONLY + [
           '-CBaseEntity:: / C_BaseEntity:: members in the DLL   0     0',
           '+CBaseEntity:: / C_BaseEntity:: members in the DLL   0     3']))
    ck('a diff with no changed row is not (nothing moved means nothing to pass)',
       not only_stock_line_counts_moved(LINES_ONLY[:4]))
    ck('CI is SOURCESHARP_CI=1 and nothing else',
       on_ci({'SOURCESHARP_CI': '1'}) and not on_ci({'SOURCESHARP_CI': '0'})
       and not on_ci({}))

    # -- the gate row parser -------------------------------------------------
    GOOD = ('1\tgate\tmembers\t10\t20\tFAIL\n'
            '3\treconcile\tToEngineIface\t5\t6\t-\n'
            '1\tverdict\tbase entity\t-\t-\tFAIL\n')
    rows = parse_gate_tsv(GOOD)
    ck('the parser keeps gate rows', ('1', 'gate', 'members') in rows)
    ck('the parser keeps reconcile rows too -- the same population, read loosely',
       ('3', 'reconcile', 'ToEngineIface') in rows)
    ck('the parser drops verdict rows, which carry no numbers', len(rows) == 2,
       'got %d' % len(rows))
    ck('a line the parser cannot read is REFUSED, not skipped',
       refused(parse_gate_tsv, '1\tgate\tshort\t3\n'))
    ck('a non-numeric count is refused', refused(parse_gate_tsv, '1\tgate\tm\tx\t2\tFAIL\n'))
    ck('a duplicated row is refused -- the baseline is keyed on it',
       refused(parse_gate_tsv, GOOD + '1\tgate\tmembers\t10\t20\tFAIL\n'))

    # -- the ratchet ---------------------------------------------------------
    base = parse_gate_tsv(GOOD)
    ck('an unchanged tree passes', not fails(quiet(gate_checks(base, base))),
       'without this the six checks below pass for the wrong reason')

    up_s = parse_gate_tsv('1\tgate\tmembers\t11\t20\tFAIL\n'
                          '3\treconcile\tToEngineIface\t5\t6\t-\n')
    got = fails(quiet(gate_checks(up_s, base)))
    ck('a SERVER number that rose fails', len(got) == 1 and 'server 10 -> 11' in got[0][1],
       repr(got))

    up_c = parse_gate_tsv('1\tgate\tmembers\t10\t21\tFAIL\n'
                          '3\treconcile\tToEngineIface\t5\t6\t-\n')
    got = fails(quiet(gate_checks(up_c, base)))
    ck('a CLIENT number that rose fails -- both columns are compared',
       len(got) == 1 and 'client 20 -> 21' in got[0][1], repr(got))

    up_r = parse_gate_tsv('1\tgate\tmembers\t10\t20\tFAIL\n'
                          '3\treconcile\tToEngineIface\t6\t6\t-\n')
    ck('a RECONCILE row that rose fails as well', len(fails(quiet(gate_checks(up_r, base)))) == 1)

    down = parse_gate_tsv('1\tgate\tmembers\t9\t20\tFAIL\n'
                          '3\treconcile\tToEngineIface\t5\t6\t-\n')
    res = quiet(gate_checks(down, base))
    ck('a number that FELL passes -- red on regression, green on progress',
       not fails(res) and any('improved' in d for _l, _ok, d in res), repr(fails(res)))

    gone = parse_gate_tsv('1\tgate\tmembers\t10\t20\tFAIL\n')
    got = fails(quiet(gate_checks(gone, base)))
    ck('a baselined row the gates stopped producing fails',
       any('did not produce' in d for _l, d in got), repr(got))

    new = parse_gate_tsv(GOOD + '3\tgate\tbrand-new-family\t7\t8\tFAIL\n')
    got = fails(quiet(gate_checks(new, base)))
    ck('a measured row the baseline does not name fails -- a regression cannot '
       'be hidden by deleting its row',
       any('does not name' in d for _l, d in got), repr(got))

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        empty = os.path.join(tmp, 'empty.tsv')
        with open(empty, 'w') as handle:
            handle.write('# only a comment\n')
        ck('an EMPTY baseline is refused, not treated as nothing to check',
           refused(read_baseline, empty))
        ck('a missing baseline is refused', refused(read_baseline, os.path.join(tmp, 'nope')))
        round_trip = os.path.join(tmp, 'rt.tsv')
        write_baseline(round_trip, base)
        ck('--write and the reader agree on the format', read_baseline(round_trip) == base,
           'a baseline this tool cannot read back is a ratchet with no floor')

    # -- the budget ----------------------------------------------------------
    live = load_live_gate()
    real = consts_from(live)
    Row = live.BudgetRow

    def row(**kw):
        d = dict(ceiling_rate=160.0, ceiling_total=1700, gated_on='total',
                 rate=100.0, total=1000, frames=10, samples=5,
                 total_min=900, total_max=1000)
        d.update(kw)
        return Row(**d)

    ck('a well-formed budget row passes', not budget_row_issues('x', row(), real),
       'without this every budget check below passes for the wrong reason')

    ck('a ceiling_total ABOVE the formula fails -- the only way §5 is defeated '
       'without a measurement',
       any('ABOVE' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(ceiling_total=1701), real)),
       repr(budget_row_issues('x', row(ceiling_total=1701), real)))
    ck('and one BELOW it passes -- live-gate keeps an unearned raise off',
       not budget_row_issues('x', row(ceiling_total=1400), real))
    ck('a ceiling_total below total_max fails on the row\'s own sample',
       any('below total_max' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(ceiling_total=900, total=900, total_min=900), real)))
    ck('a ceiling_per_frame off the formula fails',
       any('ceiling_per_frame' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(ceiling_rate=999.0), real)))
    ck('recorded_per_frame that is not total/frames fails',
       any('recorded_per_frame' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(rate=42.0, ceiling_rate=73.0), real)))
    ck('gated_on disagreeing with recorded_frames fails',
       any('gated_on' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(gated_on='rate'), real)))
    ck('recorded_total outside total_min..total_max fails',
       any('outside' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(total_max=900, ceiling_total=1550), real)))
    ck('samples of 0 fails -- a ceiling resting on no measurement',
       any('samples' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(samples=0), real)))

    # SHORT_STAGE_FLOOR raises the cap for a named stage and only for it.
    floor_stage = sorted(real.short_stage_floor)[0] if real.short_stage_floor else None
    if floor_stage:
        f = real.short_stage_floor[floor_stage]
        cap = real.ceiling_total_for(f)
        ck('SHORT_STAGE_FLOOR raises the cap for the stage it names',
           not budget_row_issues(floor_stage, row(ceiling_total=cap), real)
           and any('ABOVE' in i for i in budget_row_issues('x', row(ceiling_total=cap), real)),
           '%s floor %d -> cap %d' % (floor_stage, f, cap))

    # THE CONSTANTS ARE READ, NOT WRITTEN. Drive the same row through a stub
    # whose formulas differ and require the verdict to follow the stub: if 1.5,
    # 10 or 200 were spelled in this file the stub could not move it.
    stub = Consts(9999, {}, lambda r: 0.0, lambda t: 0)
    ck('the checks follow the constants they are GIVEN, so tools/live-gate '
       'owns 1.5, 10, 200 and MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE',
       bool(budget_row_issues('x', row(), stub))
       and not budget_row_issues('x', row(), real),
       'the same row is clean under live-gate\'s constants and dirty under a stub')

    good_budget = {'a': row(), 'b': row()}
    ck('a budget whose stages match live-gate\'s passes',
       not fails(quiet(budget_checks(good_budget, ['a', 'b'], real))))
    got = fails(quiet(budget_checks(good_budget, ['a', 'b', 'c'], real)))
    ck('a live-gate stage with no budget row fails',
       any('ungated' in d for _l, d in got), repr(got))
    got = fails(quiet(budget_checks(good_budget, ['a'], real)))
    ck('a budget row for a stage live-gate does not run fails',
       any('does not run' in d for _l, d in got), repr(got))

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        stale = os.path.join(tmp, 'six-column.tsv')
        with open(stale, 'w') as handle:
            handle.write('join\t365.929\t2691\t237.286\t1661\t7\n')
        try:
            live.read_budget(stale)
            ok = False
        except SystemExit:
            ok = True
        ck('a budget file in the OLD schema is refused by live-gate\'s own reader',
           ok, 'this tool uses that reader rather than a second copy of it')

    # -- which build is deployed ---------------------------------------------
    fx = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='entity-ratchet-')
    try:
        def edge(target, out, dest):
            return ('build %s: CXX_SHARED_LIBRARY_LINKER__%s_Debug a.o\n'
                    '  POST_BUILD = cd x && cmake -DIN=%s -DDEST_DIR=%s -P deploy.cmake\n\n'
                    % (out, target, out, dest))
        with open(os.path.join(fx, 'build.ninja'), 'w') as handle:
            handle.write(edge('server_sourcesharp', 'out/server.so', '/d')
                         + 'build out/x.o: CXX_COMPILER__server_sourcesharp_Debug x.cpp\n'
                         + edge('client_sourcesharp', 'out/client.so', '/d')
                         + edge('sourcesharp', 'out/libmanaged_host.so', '/d'))
        edges = deploy_edges(fx)
        ck('the deploy edges come out of build.ninja: link output -> DEST_DIR',
           edges == [('/d/server.so', os.path.join(fx, 'out/server.so')),
                     ('/d/client.so', os.path.join(fx, 'out/client.so')),
                     ('/d/libmanaged_host.so', os.path.join(fx, 'out/libmanaged_host.so'))],
           repr(edges))
        ck('a compile edge is not a deploy edge -- only a link edge names a deployment',
           len(edges) == 3)
        with open(os.path.join(fx, 'build.ninja'), 'w') as handle:
            handle.write(edge('server_sourcesharp', 'out/server.so', '/d'))
        ck('a build with a deployed target missing is refused, not half-checked',
           refused(deploy_edges, fx))
    finally:
        import shutil
        shutil.rmtree(fx)
    ck('matching build-ids pass',
       check_deployed_is_this_build(edges, lambda p: 'deadbeef') == 3)
    ck('a DEPLOYED binary from a different build is refused, not compared',
       refused(check_deployed_is_this_build, edges,
               lambda p: 'aaaa' if p.startswith('/d/') else 'bbbb'))
    ck('a binary with no build-id is refused',
       refused(check_deployed_is_this_build, edges, lambda p: None))
    ck('an empty deploy list is refused, not passed as "nothing to compare"',
       refused(check_deployed_is_this_build, [], lambda p: 'x'))

    # -- and the fixtures are not the tree -----------------------------------
    #
    # Every check above ran against a stand-in, and a stand-in drifts: rename a
    # column tomorrow and all of them still pass while the real files stop
    # being read at all. So the last block asks the REAL tree.
    src = open(os.path.join(ROOT, LIVE_GATE), encoding='utf-8').read()
    spelled = re.search(r'^MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE = (\d+)$', src, re.M)
    ck('the imported MIN_FRAMES_FOR_RATE is the one tools/live-gate spells',
       bool(spelled) and int(spelled.group(1)) == real.min_frames_for_rate,
       'imported %s, source says %s' % (real.min_frames_for_rate,
                                        spelled.group(1) if spelled else 'nothing'))

    try:
        real_base = read_baseline(os.path.join(ROOT, BASELINE))
    except CannotMeasure as exc:
        real_base = {}
        print('  (baseline unreadable: %s)' % exc)
    ck('the REAL ratchet baseline parses and names a serious number of rows',
       len(real_base) >= 20, '%d rows' % len(real_base))

    real_edges = deploy_edges(os.path.join(FARM, BUILD))
    ck('the REAL build names its deployments and they carry this '
       'build\'s id',
       bool(real_edges) and check_deployed_is_this_build(real_edges, build_id)
       == len(real_edges), '%d deploy edges' % len(real_edges))

    real_budget = live.read_budget(live.BUDGET)
    stages = [s[0] for s in live.ALL_STEPS]
    ck('the REAL budget covers every live-gate stage and breaks no invariant',
       not fails(quiet(budget_checks(real_budget, stages, real))),
       '%d rows, %d stages' % (len(real_budget), len(stages)))

    # The fixture-summary guard. report() above wrote lines that look exactly
    # like production ones; they must have been HELD, not printed, or
    # tools/run-test-legs would sum a fixture's "2 failed" into this leg. The
    # count must be NON-ZERO: a guard that passes when nothing was captured is
    # the vacuous green this file is against.
    held = sum(len(re.findall(r'^  ==> \d+ checks?, \d+ failed$', t, re.M))
               for t in transcripts)
    ck('the fixture summaries were CAPTURED, not left on stdout', held > 0,
       '%d held across %d fixture passes' % (held, len(transcripts)))

    print('  ==> %d checks, %d failed' % (checks, failed))
    return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    if '--self-test' in sys.argv[1:]:
        os.chdir(FARM)
        sys.exit(self_test())
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
